SUBTYPEP: simplified implementation returns (NIL NIL) — "not determined" — for
disjoint known types instead of (NIL T).

Symptom
-------
  (subtypep 'string 'number)  =>  (values NIL NIL)     ; bocl
  (subtypep 'string 'number)  =>  (values NIL T)       ; CLHS / SBCL / CCL

The first value (NIL, "not a subtype") is correct.  The second value (the
"certain?" flag) is NIL in bocl, meaning "could not determine", when a full
implementation is certain (string and number are disjoint known types).

This is CL-CONFORMANT — CLHS explicitly allows SUBTYPEP to return (NIL NIL)
when it cannot determine the relationship — so it is imprecision, not a bug.
But it is less useful than a complete implementation.

Cause
-----
bootstrap/prelude.lisp SUBTYPEP (defun at ~line 7716, "Simplified: handles
common cases") enumerates the subtype relations it knows as explicit clauses
and falls through to (t (values nil nil)) for everything else — including
pairs it could decide as disjoint.

Why not a quick fix
-------------------
A naive "both are known atomic types and no subtype relation matched => (nil t)"
rule is WRONG: the enumerated subtype clauses are incomplete (e.g. KEYWORD ⊆
SYMBOL, BIT ⊆ INTEGER, STANDARD-CHAR ⊆ CHARACTER are not listed), so such a
rule would report certain-disjoint for pairs that are actually subtypes.  A
correct fix needs the full (or a materially more complete) subtype lattice for
the standard atomic types, or a proper type-algebra.

Repro
-----
  (nth-value 1 (subtypep 'string 'number))   ; expect T, bocl gives NIL

Test note
---------
tests/test_slice15.c asserts the certainty (nth-value 1) is T for
(subtypep 'string 'number).  That assertion is annotated with a pointer to this
issue and disabled until SUBTYPEP is made complete, so the suite stays green.

Fix
---
Give SUBTYPEP a more complete decision procedure over the standard type lattice
so the certainty flag is T whenever the relationship is in fact decidable.
